WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
You run your package managers. The terminal spits out a wall of text, and finally, you see that bright green bar of success.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:13.000
It is very tempting to look at those terminal windows and assume your React Native upgrade is finished.

3
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.500
But a successful yarn install or pod install is a false positive.

4
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:22.000
All that green text means is that your local environment resolved its dependency tree.

5
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.500
It tells you nothing about whether the application will actually build or run.

6
00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:33.500
Upgrading React Native means swapping out the underlying native bridge and shifting the actual compilation artifacts.

7
00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:39.500
To know if those architecture changes are stable, you have to execute a completely fresh native compilation.

8
00:00:39.500 --> 00:00:45.500
When you try to compile, you expose the first category of post-upgrade breaks, build time failures.

9
00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:53.500
These are deep configuration errors, like a mismatched Gradle plugin version on Android, or a CocoaCode's resolution conflict on iOS.

10
00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:57.500
If the build does succeed, you then face runtime crashes.

11
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:05.500
These errors only trigger the moment you actually launch the app, caused by a JSI-type mismatch or a native module failing to load.

12
00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:16.000
Finally, there are behavioral regressions. The app compiles and it runs, but a critical navigation gesture is broken, or a screen layout overflows the edges of the device.

13
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.500
Works on my machine is not a validation strategy.

14
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:26.500
To ship an architecture change safely, you need a deterministic, multi-platform pipeline that starts from a completely clean slate.

15
00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:30.500
That clean slate begins with invalidating the build caches.

16
00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:36.500
Cached artifacts from your previous React Native version are the primary cause of non-deterministic build outcomes.

17
00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:39.500
If you leave old caches in place, you risk two outcomes.

18
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:44.500
You get a false positive where the build passes only because it relies on outdated files.

19
00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:51.500
Or you get a false negative, a build that fails purely because of a stale reference, not because your updated code is broken.

20
00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:54.500
This is a structural layout of an Xcode project.

21
00:01:54.500 --> 00:02:03.500
The red lines represent how stale artifacts from the previous React Native version remain tightly injected directly into the project's references.

22
00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:06.500
You cannot simply build over these.

23
00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:13.500
Running the pod deintegrate command physically severs these stale pod injected references from the Xcode project file.

24
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:19.500
It leaves the main project node completely isolated. Following that with a fresh pod install,

25
00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:25.500
deterministically rebuilds those connections, strictly pulling from your newly updated pod file.

26
00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:34.500
Executing a strict cache purge is the only way to ensure that the errors you encounter in the next phase, Native compilation, are real, actionable problems.

27
00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:39.500
Now you can move to compiling the Native Android release build entirely from source.

28
00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:45.500
This table details the most common Android compilation failures that emerge after a major architecture upgrade.

29
00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:51.500
If you hit a Gradle plugin mismatch, it means your plugin version is out of sync with your Gradle wrapper.

30
00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:56.500
You resolve this by aligning both versions against the React Native upgrade helper diff.

31
00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:59.500
Next are missing namespace errors.

32
00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:06.500
This requires opening the build.gradle file and explicitly defining the Android.namespace string for your app.

33
00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:12.500
If you are moving to React Native 0.76 or higher and targeting the new architecture,

34
00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:18.500
you will encounter sttay errors unless you bump your min SDK version to 24.

35
00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:23.500
You may also see runtime errors claiming a module is missing from the turbo module registry.

36
00:03:23.500 --> 00:03:30.500
This happens when native modules are registered under the wrong name string or completely omitted from the get packages list.

37
00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:36.500
Finally, CMake and JNI build failures usually stem from stale cogen outputs.

38
00:03:36.500 --> 00:03:41.500
You have to purge those artifacts and regenerate them directly from the schema before rebuilding.

39
00:03:41.500 --> 00:03:47.500
These are deep native layer compilation errors. JavaScript only validation tools will never catch them.

40
00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:51.500
The parallel process is compiling the iOS release build from source.

41
00:03:51.500 --> 00:03:56.500
We have a similar table for the specific iOS compilation failures that surface post upgrade.

42
00:03:56.500 --> 00:04:05.500
A missing rctapplicate.h file error requires you to explicitly add the react rctapplicate pod import into your main application.

43
00:04:05.500 --> 00:04:10.500
Cocoa pods version conflicts happen because local Ruby gem environments fall behind.

44
00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:16.500
You will likely need to manually update your local cocoa pods installation to meet the new react native requirements.

45
00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:21.500
If you get xcode build phase conflicts, check for duplicate info.plist files.

46
00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:28.500
These must be manually removed through the target settings. Starting with 0.76, you may hit Swift compiler errors.

47
00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:36.500
Because the underlying rctapplicate has changed, your custom application override signatures will no longer match and require manual refactoring.

48
00:04:36.500 --> 00:04:43.500
Achieving a clean, successful native build on both Android and iOS proves that your code compiles structurally.

49
00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:48.500
But compilation does not guarantee behavioral or runtime stability.

50
00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:54.500
At this stage, your automated unit tests might fail. Post upgrade, this rarely means your logic is broken.

51
00:04:54.500 --> 00:05:01.500
It usually means a mocked native module is missing a new method or a snapshot test is simply capturing deprecated behavior.

52
00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:06.500
To find the real runtime errors, you have to execute a physical device smoke test.

53
00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:10.500
Emulators and simulators are inadequate for architecture validation.

54
00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:15.500
At a hardware level, emulators bypass actual network latency by using the host machine's connection,

55
00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:21.500
and they cannot accurately replicate the exact CPU and GPU constraints of physical hardware.

56
00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:30.500
They also mask real memory pressure, fail to test hardware APIs like biometrics properly, and execute Hermes garbage collection differently than a mobile processor.

57
00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:36.500
This split matrix details the exact physical hardware tiers required to surface those blind spots.

58
00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:46.500
On the Android side, testing on a low-end device with three gigabytes of RAM or less is mandatory to expose hidden memory leaks and start-up time regressions.

59
00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:56.500
You also need a mid-range device to represent your median user, and an older device running API24 to verify your absolute minimum baseline still operates.

60
00:05:56.500 --> 00:06:05.500
Testing on Android 15 is specifically required to catch edge-case layout overflows caused by the new gesture navigation bar behaviors.

61
00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:19.500
For the iOS matrix, you must validate against the absolute newest iOS to test features like Dynamic Island APIs, and contrast that with an older iOS version to ensure your deployment target baseline hasn't broken.

62
00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:27.500
Only a rigorous matrix of physical hardware can expose the complex behavioral regressions introduced by a deep framework upgrade.

63
00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:38.500
Once established, this entire sequence, clearing caches, native compilation, and hardware matrices, must be codified into an automated CI pipeline.

64
00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:45.500
Automating these checks is how you prevent framework level regressions from silently creeping into future, smaller feature updates.

65
00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:52.500
But a cleanly tested build is still only a baseline. It is not a guarantee of zero production impact.

66
00:06:52.500 --> 00:07:04.500
In the past, we have tracked a 48-hour post-release crash rate, abnormal spikes, trigger remediation, JS crashes demand code push rollbacks, while native crashes require immediate app store hot fixes.

67
00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:17.500
By abandoning the false positive of a successful local install, and treating react native upgrades as a rigorous, hardware verified pipeline, engineering teams can execute architecture changes with absolute confidence.

